---
/**
 * The consent banner. First visit, every page, nothing loading behind it.
 *
 * THE COPY BELOW IS THE APPROVED 27 AUGUST 2026 TEXT WITH ONE SENTENCE CHANGED, AND THE
 * CHANGE WAS FORCED RATHER THAN CHOSEN. The approved version ends "You can turn them off
 * at any time in Cookie settings". Later the same day Fredrick instructed that analytics
 * be always on and not switchable, which made that sentence false about one of the three
 * categories it covers. A banner cannot say a thing the site does not do, so the close
 * now names the two categories that can actually be turned off, and adds a short line
 * saying analytics is always on. Everything else — the opening, the purposes, all five
 * provider names, the Privacy Policy reference — is untouched.
 *
 * CONSENT_COPY_VERSION WAS BUMPED IN THE SAME CHANGE, so every record stored against the
 * earlier wording is treated as stale and those visitors are asked again. That is the
 * mechanism working: a stored record is agreement to a specific sentence, and this is a
 * different sentence.
 *
 * THIS COPY NEEDS RE-APPROVAL. It was edited under instruction, not signed off as
 * written. Beyond that, DO NOT EDIT IT — not to tighten it, not to remove the two
 * providers it names that are not loading in this release, not for house style.
 *
 * IT NAMES FIVE PROVIDERS AND THREE ARE LOADING. LinkedIn and Google Ads are built as
 * inert slots in this release. Naming them here is deliberate and it is the safe
 * direction: a banner that over-discloses asks for more than it takes, and the day
 * either slot is switched on, no visitor has to be re-prompted. Under-disclosing is the
 * failure that matters, and this cannot do it.
 *
 * ACCEPT-ONLY: one button, no decline. For advertising and session recording, declining
 * is not absent — it is the default, and it is what happens for a visitor who ignores
 * this entirely. Neither loads until the button is pressed. That is why the Cookie
 * settings panel is not optional: it is the whole of the withdrawal mechanism, and the
 * banner points at it in its own copy.
 *
 * ANALYTICS IS THE EXCEPTION AND THE BANNER SAYS SO. It has already loaded by the time
 * this is on screen. Pressing Accept does not start it and ignoring this does not stop
 * it, which is precisely why the copy states it plainly instead of leaving the reader to
 * infer it from a panel they may never open.
 *
 * IT IS NOT A MODAL, and that is a decision. It does not trap focus and it does not
 * cover the page, because nothing behind it is loading and there is nothing to protect
 * the visitor from. A visitor who wants to read the site and never answer this can Tab
 * straight past it and will be tracked by nothing. A modal would be theatre — it would
 * imply the site is withholding something until they agree, which it is not.
 */
---

<div
  class="consent-banner"
  id="ark-consent-banner"
  role="region"
  aria-label="Cookies"
  hidden
>
  <div class="consent-banner__inner">
    <div class="consent-banner__copy">
      <p class="consent-banner__title">We use cookies.</p>
      <p class="consent-banner__text">
        We use cookies for analytics, advertising and session recording &mdash; Google
        Analytics, Meta, LinkedIn, Google Ads and Microsoft Clarity &mdash; to understand
        how the site is used and to measure our advertising. Analytics is always on. See our <a
          href="/privacy"
          class="consent-banner__link">Privacy Policy</a
        >. You can turn advertising and session recording off at any time in <button
          type="button"
          class="consent-banner__link consent-banner__link--button"
          data-cookie-settings>Cookie settings</button
        >.
      </p>
    </div>

    <button type="button" class="consent-banner__accept" data-consent-accept>
      Accept
    </button>
  </div>
</div>

<style>
  /*
   * Pinned to the bottom, above everything. It is a functional element: one surface, one
   * hairline, one shadow, no illustration and no colour it does not need. The page's own
   * card treatment, not a new one.
   */
  .consent-banner {
    position: fixed;
    inset-inline: 0;
    bottom: 0;
    z-index: 200;
    background: var(--ark-white);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--ark-hairline);
    box-shadow: var(--ark-floating);
  }

  .consent-banner[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }

  .consent-banner__inner {
    width: 100%;
    max-width: calc(1264px + var(--ark-gutter) * 2);
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding: 20px var(--ark-gutter);
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 32px;
  }

  .consent-banner__title {
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--ark-ink);
  }

  /*
   * The measure sits on the element carrying the font-size, never on a wrapper — `ch`
   * resolves against the element's own size, and on a wrapper it would count 16px
   * characters and cramp this to a fraction of its width.
   */
  .consent-banner__text {
    max-width: 96ch;
    margin-top: 4px;
    font-size: var(--ark-small);
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--ark-body-ink);
  }

  .consent-banner__link {
    color: var(--ark-olive);
    font-weight: 600;
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
  }

  /* "Cookie settings" opens a panel, so it is a button. It is set to read as the link
   * beside it because the sentence treats them as the same kind of thing. */
  .consent-banner__link--button {
    padding: 0;
    border: 0;
    background: none;
    font: inherit;
    font-weight: 600;
    text-decoration: underline;
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  /* `primary` fill, 10px radius, label size — the design system's button, unchanged. */
  .consent-banner__accept {
    flex: none;
    padding: 15px 40px;
    border: 1px solid transparent;
    border-radius: var(--ark-radius-sm);
    background: var(--ark-olive);
    color: var(--ark-on-dark);
    font-family: var(--ark-font-body);
    font-size: var(--ark-label);
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.2;
    cursor: pointer;
    transition: background-color 0.15s ease;
  }

  .consent-banner__accept:hover {
    background: #2c401f;
  }

  /* base.css carries no site-wide focus ring — only the skip link has one — so every
   * control in this release draws its own. A consent banner that cannot be operated
   * visibly by keyboard is a consent banner that does not work. */
  .consent-banner :focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--ark-olive);
    outline-offset: 2px;
    border-radius: 3px;
  }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .consent-banner__inner {
      flex-direction: column;
      align-items: stretch;
      gap: 16px;
      padding: 18px var(--ark-gutter-phone);
    }

    /* Full width on a phone, where a button sized to its label reads as an aside. */
    .consent-banner__accept {
      width: 100%;
      padding: 15px 22px;
    }
  }
</style>
